verdict.reason:
Re-read from raw source, both cited locations exist exactly as claimed and there is no other 2FA/TOTP check anywhere on this path.

connection.rs:1619 — `send_logon_response_and_keep_alive()`:
```
if self.require_2fa.is_some() && !self.is_recent_session(true) && !self.from_switch {
    ... generate TOTP code, send REQUIRE_2FA, return true (wait for Auth2fa) ...
}
...
self.authorized = true;   // line 1657
```
`!self.from_switch` is a bare OR-term in the guard: whenever `from_switch == true` the whole require_2fa block is skipped unconditionally, regardless of `is_recent_session`, and execution falls straight through to `self.authorized = true` at 1657. No alternate 2FA/TOTP verification exists anywhere in this function.

`from_switch` is set in exactly one place, the `SwitchSidesResponse` arm of `on_message` (2806-2834), confirmed line-for-line:
```
} else if let Some(message::Union::SwitchSidesResponse(_s)) = msg.union {
    ...
    self.handle_login_request_without_validation(&lr).await;   // 2810 — just copies lr, no auth
    let uuid_old = SWITCH_SIDES_UUID.lock().unwrap().remove(&lr.my_id);   // 2815
    if let Ok(uuid) = uuid::Uuid::from_slice(...) {
        if let Some((_instant, uuid_old)) = uuid_old {
            if uuid == uuid_old {
                self.from_switch = true;                                  // 2819
                self.set_conn_audit_primary_auth(...SwitchSides);
                if !self.send_logon_response_and_keep_alive().await { ... } // 2821
```
This arm is reached before `self.authorized` is set (it sits among the pre-authorization `else if` branches of `on_message`, confirmed at 2506-2523), and `handle_login_request_without_validation` (2449-2470) — the only other function touched on this path — contains solely the separate, already-known hwid/trusted-device bypass (2455-2467), not a TOTP check. So the only gate standing between an incoming `SwitchSidesResponse` and full authorization is a 122-bit uuid match against `SWITCH_SIDES_UUID`; TOTP is never asked for or verified on this path. This is a genuinely distinct code path/mechanism from the seed hwid-trusted-device bypass (different map, different trigger, different function), so it is a legitimate new sibling, not a re-report.

I additionally traced the full switch-sides protocol (connection.rs 3538-3554/5789-5815, client.rs 3485-3703, ipc.rs 968-989) to check for a hidden compensating control. There isn't one that re-verifies the bypassed party's own 2FA: the whole mechanism is a single ephemeral-uuid round trip that the switch-INITIATING party (the one whose require_2fa ends up skipped) generates on her own machine and echoes back to herself via the peer — it is symmetric/parallel-gated with `is_recent_session(true)` (a "recently 2FA'd" cache), which does suggest the developers treated `from_switch` as a deliberate, audited alternate trust signal (there's a dedicated `ConnAuditPrimaryAuth::SwitchSides` enum for it) rather than a pure oversight — but "logged as a distinct auth method" is not a mitigating check, and I found no code that requires a fresh TOTP proof on the switch-back leg. The 2FA gate is observably absent at the Rust layer, exactly as claimed.

One clarification worth noting on the attack narrative directionality: the party whose `require_2fa` is bypassed is the party who *herself* clicked "switch sides" while she was the controller of the peer (she generated and owns the uuid in her own `SWITCH_SIDES_UUID` map, keyed by the peer's id) — not an independent "Party B [who] previously completed a normal password(+2FA) login to A" as phrased. The uuid never leaves the two peers already engaged in that live, already-authenticated session, so this is closer to a self-inflicted/social-engineerable role-reversal that silently drops the switch-initiator's own 2FA requirement, than a bypass usable by an unrelated remote party — this matches (and is already captured by) the candidate's own confidence-hedge and disposition_hint, so it does not change the verdict, just refines the "attack" prose.
